# T7-001 -- warm-start stability (plan v2.0.1 §4.6)
#
# A compositor drops a layer from the middle of its stack. The stability
# bonus in Allocator::score_pair is supposed to leave every surviving layer
# on the plane it already held, so the frame after the removal should
# reprogram nothing except the plane being vacated.
#
# This is the scenario most likely to diverge silently. The matcher
# maximizes cardinality first and uses score only to order tie-breaks, so a
# port that drops the bonus still places every layer and still passes every
# "did it composite correctly" test -- it just picks different planes each
# time the layer set changes, and the display flickers. Only a
# property-write trace shows it.
#
# The reference's answer, for the record: ov2 stays on plane[2] rather than
# compacting down into the hole at plane[1], the vacated plane is explicitly
# disabled, and no new TEST_ONLY commit is issued at all -- the warm
# allocation is reused minus the removed layer.
#
# Note this does NOT re-split the backing store, which is how the plan words
# §4.6. On vkms that phrasing cannot hold: its planes expose no zpos
# property, so stacking order is fixed by plane index, and adding a layer
# below the overlays forces every one of them to shift up. Removing a layer
# from the middle tests the same bonus without the constraint dominating the
# result. See docs/parity-findings.md.
#
# Grammar (one command per line, '#' comments, blank lines ignored):
#   add NAME ZPOS DST_X DST_Y W H   -- W H of 0 0 means the full display
#   del NAME
#   frame                           -- build + real commit + land the flip

add bg  0 0 0 0 0
add ov1 1 64 64 256 256
add ov2 2 400 200 256 256

# Two steady frames: the first carries the modeset, the second warms the
# allocation cache.
frame
frame

# Drop the middle layer. The two survivors must not move.
del ov1
frame
frame
